Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:39:54 +0800
Subject: [05/14] v1.6 at 3 eV/ps: SNR improves but the interface drifts
 (negative result)

Hypothesis: with an annealed interface the flux could be raised to 3 eV/ps to
gain a 3x signal-to-noise ratio without the drift seen before annealing.

Result (run_v040/v041, same v1.6 protocol):
  flat: TBR 4.86e-9 +- 0.19 (3.9 %), time conv 29.2 %, drift +0.91 K
  P350: TBR 4.29e-9 +- 0.17 (4.0 %), time conv 23.0 %, drift +3.03 K
The relative uncertainty does drop to 4 %, but the half-to-half difference is
8 sigma, i.e. a real drift: the high flux accelerates interface evolution even
on an annealed structure. 1 eV/ps stays the operating point.

Consequently the only remaining way to reach time_converged <= 5 % at 1 eV/ps
is statistics: 1.6 ns of measured production (runs run_v042/v043, in progress),
which should bring each half to sigma ~3.5 %.
